# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.metaldev).
#
# The first question of subsystem (2), the Metal object graph: can this port make a
# subclass of Metal's private _MTLDevice, and what does its -initWithAcceleratorPort:
# do when handed this port's own IOAcceleratorES service?
#
# docs/metal.md section 1 read the five steps Metal takes to find a GPU out of
# Metal.framework. Steps 4 and 5 are the ones that decide whether a driver of ours is
# possible at all: the class must be a subclass of _MTLDevice, and the object comes from
# -initWithAcceleratorPort:. src/metal_dev.m walks those two by hand, in its own process,
# so that a crash costs a line in this log instead of the window server.
#
# It deliberately does NOT give the service the two properties (MetalPluginName,
# MetalPluginClassName) that would make CoreAnimation and OpenGL ES take the Metal road on
# the next boot: section 7 measured that road as fatal while there is nothing behind it.
#
# No GPU is needed for this: the service exists whether or not the Adreno came up, and
# nothing here draws.
/redwood/bin/metal_dev
#
# Then the experiment metal_dev's answer made possible. Apple's IOGPUMetalDevice init
# refused with an assertion -- it requires the subclass to conform to MTLDeviceSPI -- and
# that gate is in userland, where class_addProtocol passes it. metal_stubdev builds such a
# subclass, stubs every method those protocols require with one that prints its own name,
# and lets Apple's initialisation interrogate it. The log is then the order of the
# questions a driver of ours has to answer, measured rather than guessed.
/redwood/bin/metal_stubdev IOGPUMetalDevice
#
# the same count for a direct subclass of _MTLDevice, which is the size of route A: how
# many of the methods MTLDeviceSPI and MTLDevice require are answered by nothing in the
# chain. (Its step 5 is expected to throw: _MTLDevice's init is the abstract stub.)
/redwood/bin/metal_stubdev _MTLDevice
#
# the same against the legacy path, which refused by returning nil rather than by asserting
/redwood/bin/metal_stubdev MTLIOAccelDevice
#
# And the experiment that decides the first milestone. Reversing MTLAddDevice gave a
# contract of three methods and two protocol declarations for an object Metal will accept;
# this replays that sequence on a subclass of _MTLDevice that has exactly those, once per
# candidate featureProfile value, and then hands the survivor to Metal's own MTLAddDevice
# and asks MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() for a device. Discovery is bypassed and said to
# be bypassed; registration is not.
/redwood/bin/metal_stubdev _MTLDevice --minimal
#
# And the same question asked of the real driver library rather than of classes assembled at
# runtime: does a source-declared subclass of the private _MTLDevice link and load here, and
# does Metal take it? (src/redwoodmetal.m, src/metal_drv_test.m)
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test
#
# And the first dispatch worth trying: MetalFaceMask_InitMinMax takes exactly one device
# buffer (24 bytes, read_write, `atomicMinMax`) and no textures or samplers, which is what
# this driver can bind today; it also writes its buffer unconditionally, so an all-zero
# result afterwards means the dispatch did not happen rather than that the kernel had
# nothing to say. tools/air/kstruct.py sorted the corpus's 47 kernels for exactly this.
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test /System/Library/CoreImage/PortraitFilters.cifilter/default.metallib MetalFaceMask_InitMinMax
#
# And the same driver reached the way Metal's own discovery reaches a plug-in: through
# NSBundle, out of a flat unsigned bundle of ours. This is the one part of discovery that
# cannot be settled by reading the loader -- whether -[NSBundle classNamed:] gives up our
# class, since it tests that the class came out of that very bundle. Nothing is published to
# the service, so Metal is not asked to find anything (docs/metal.md 10.14).
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --bundle
#
# and what the system believed about Metal before this, for the contrast
/redwood/bin/metal_test
